  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 1021 PM MST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nAbove-normal temperatures and dry conditions will prevail through \nmuch of the work week. Weak upper level ridging will be near the \narea as a strong upper low sits to our east and a trough moves onto \nthe West Coast Wednesday. For Thanksgiving, temperatures will be in \nthe upper 40s to mid 50s for the plains and the 30s and 40s for the \nhigher terrain. Winds will be on the lighter side for the plains \n(generally less than 15mph) with mostly sunny skies on Thursday. \nTemperatures will peak on Friday with highs in the low 60s for much \nof the plains. \n\nOn Friday, an upper level low is forecast to develop to our \nnorth/northeast over Canada and a strong high is forecast to develop \nto our southwest over the Pacific. This will put us in an area of \nstrong northwest winds aloft. Winds will start to pick up in the \nhigher terrain overnight Thursday into Friday, peaking Saturday \nmorning. Stronger winds could make it onto the plains on Saturday\nas a cold front is dragged through the area. However, there is \nstill a lot of model uncertainty with the system this weekend. The\nECMWF is still showing a more potent shortwave moving through on \nSaturday with a strong cold front. While the GFS has a much more \nsubtle shortwave and weaker front. Other models are more on board \nwith the Euro than the GFS. Also, the GFS is trending slightly \nstronger with the front in latest runs. This has us leaning more \ntowards the stronger front and better precip chances solution fort\nhis weekend. Confidence is high in snow falling in our northern \nmountains, with decreasing confidence as we go further south and \nfurther east on the plains. Chances for snow on the plains will \ndepend on the shortwave and timing/strength of the front, with \nthe current NBM at a low to medium chance (10% to 40%) for at \nleast a trace of snow across the plains by Sunday morning. \n\nTemperatures will drop closer to normal for the weekend, with highs \nin the 40s and low 50s. Forecast uncertainty continues for early \nnext week as models show very different solutions on the potential \nprogression of another shortwave and front Monday.",
